libFuzzer targets for Small Python, after QuickJS's fuzz/ directory.

  fuzz_eval     executes the input as a module: lexer, parser, compiler,
                and runtime together.
  fuzz_compile  passes the input to compile() as bytes without running it:
                source decoding, lexer, parser, and compiler. The first byte
                picks the mode (exec, eval, or single).
  fuzz_spyc     bundles the input with spyc's own code: import discovery,
                the module search, --minify, and the generated C, metafile,
                and dependency rule. The input is a small tree split at each
                line "#--" (main.py, a.py, pkg/__init__.py, pkg/b.py),
                written to a fresh directory under /tmp; the first byte
                picks spyc's options.
  fuzz_oom      runs the input as fuzz_eval does, with one
                allocation failing: the first two bytes pick which one after
                context creation, and whether every later one fails too, as
                tests/oom does for each in turn. The run must free
                everything it allocated.

For fuzz_eval and fuzz_compile, each input gets a fresh context with the standard layer (spy_std_init), no
output, no input, no module loader, and no mounts, and a step limit so loops
end. The context is collected and freed after every input, so AddressSanitizer
reports leaks and use-after-free per input.

Building needs a clang with libFuzzer. Apple clang has none; use Homebrew's
LLVM, linking its libc++:

  make libfuzzer
  make libfuzzer FUZZ_CC=$(brew --prefix llvm)/bin/clang \
      LIB_FUZZING_ENGINE="-fsanitize=fuzzer,address,undefined \
        -L$(brew --prefix llvm)/lib/c++ -Wl,-rpath,$(brew --prefix llvm)/lib/c++"

Where AddressSanitizer does not start (it hangs on recent macOS), build and
run in Linux instead: docker run with python:3.14 and apt-get install clang,
then plain make libfuzzer.

make fuzz builds all four and runs each for FUZZ_SECONDS (default 60) on a corpus
seeded from the examples and language tests, in fork mode so out-of-memory
and slow inputs are not reported as findings; crashes land in .obj/fuzz/.
By hand:

  mkdir -p corpus
  for f in examples/*.py tests/spec/*/*.py; do cp $f corpus/$(echo $f | tr / _); done
  ./fuzz/fuzz_eval -dict=fuzz/fuzz.dict -timeout=10 -rss_limit_mb=2048 corpus
  ./fuzz/fuzz_compile -dict=fuzz/fuzz.dict corpus

make fuzz-replay runs that corpus again through fuzz/replay.c, a driver
without libFuzzer, under REPLAY_SANITIZERS (default memory), so every kept
input is also checked for reads of uninitialized memory. It needs clang.

Reproduce a crash with ./fuzz/fuzz_eval crash-<hash>. Regenerate fuzz.dict
from the engine's builtins after `make spy` with python3 fuzz/generate_dict.py.

The engine has no memory limit, so an input like 'x' * 10**10 is reported as
an out-of-memory; -rss_limit_mb and -malloc_limit_mb set where that happens.
